.\" @(#)chapter.2 1.1 85/08/09 SMI;
.\" Needs correct page headers and footers
.LP
.\"	.PL RIGHT
.ps 20
.ce 10
Sun Network Services
.ce 0
.ps 11
.LP
This chapter introduces the Sun Network services. 
We describe the services currently available, and define
some terms in the network environment.
.LP
Following that, we introduce and explain each of the services now available for the Sun \*(Un
workstation: network file system service and
yellow pages service. 
Within each of these sections you will find
information about periodic maintenance and trouble-shooting for the
service under discussion.
.LP
While some of this material tends to be theoretical, its specific
implications will be seen again and again as you become familiar
with system administration. 
For example, if you run the
yellow pages, you must understand that some typical \*(Un
procedures have changed in the yellow pages environment.
That is also true if you use the network file
system. 
This chapter covers only those aspects of network services
necessary for performing the duties of system administration. 
For a complete theoretical overview, see \fIThe Network Services Guide\fP.
.LP
At the end of this chapter we explain how to add new users and new
client machines at your site, one of the first and most important
duties of system administration.
.bp
.NH
Introduction And Terminology
.NH 2
Networking Models
.LP
There are many ways to make computers and networks interface
transparently. 
The two major ones are the distributed operating
system approach and the network services approach.
.LP
A distributed operating system allows the network software designer 
to make grand assumptions about the other machines on the network.
Two of these assumptions are usually: that the remote piece of hardware
is identical to the local hardware, and that the remote and local
machines are running identical software. 
These assumptions allow a
quick and simple implementation of a network system in an environment
limited to specific hardware and software. 
This type of distributed
operating system is, by design, closed. 
That is to say, it's very
difficult to integrate new hardware or software into a closed
network environment, unless it comes from the vendor of that network
system. 
A closed network system forces a customer to return to one
vendor for solutions to all computing needs.
.LP
On the other hand, the Sun network is not closed. 
Sun bases its networking on network services. 
Network service is
made up of remote programs composed of remote procedures called
from the network.  
Optimally, a remote procedure computes results based
entirely on it's own parameters. 
Thus, the procedure (and therefore,
the network service) is not tied to any particular operating system or
hardware. 
The design of the Sun network makes it possible for a variety
of machines and software to talk to the network service, enabling the
Sun Workstation to talk to various types of computers.
.LP
As you might expect, the Sun network services are more complex in
design and implementation than a closed distributed operating
system. 
Since remote procedures are operating-system independent and
hardware independent, multiple remote procedures must sometimes be
called in the Sun environment, where a single transaction might
suffice in a closed system.
.NH 2
Terminology
.LP
The bulk of this chapter explains administration of two Sun network
services \(em the Sun Network File System (\s-2NFS\s0), 
and the Sun Yellow Pages (\fLyp\fP\|). 
Before discussing
these two services, we define some generic concepts and terms. 
.LP
Any machine that provides one of these network services is a server.
A single machine may provide more than one service. 
In fact, a typical
configuration would be for a single machine to act as both
an \s-2NFS\s0-server, and a \fLyp\fP-server.
.LP
In each of the Sun network services, servers are entirely passive.
The servers wait for clients to call them, they never call the clients.
.LP
A client is any entity that accesses a network service.
We use the term entity because the thing doing the accessing may be
an actual machine or simply a \*(Un
process generated by a piece of software.
.bp
.LP
The degree to which clients are bound to their server varies with each
of the Sun network services. 
For example, in \fLnd\fP service the
\fLnd\fP client is strictly bound to the \fLnd\fP server because the
server supplies a private area of a large disk for the exclusive use
of each client. 
At the other extreme, a Sun \fLyp\fP client binds
randomly to one of the \fLyp\fP servers by broadcasting a request. 
At any point, the \fLyp\fP client may decide to broadcast for a new
server. 
The Sun \s-2NFS\s0
stands somewhere in the middle.  
An \s-2NFS\s0 client may choose to mount file systems from 
any number of servers at any time.
.LP
In all cases, however, the client initiates the binding. 
The server completes the binding subject to access control 
rules specific to each service. 
Since most network administration problems occur at bind
time, a system administrator should know how a client binds to a server
and what (if any) access control policy each server uses.
.NH 2
UNIX Meets Sun Network Services
.LP
Unlike many recently marketed distributed operating systems, \*(Un
was originally designed without knowledge that networks existed. 
This ``networking ignorance'' presents three impediments to linking \*(Un
with currently available high performance networks:
.IP 1)
\*(Un was never designed to yield to a higher
authority (like a network authentication server) for critical
information or services.  
As a result some \*(Un
semantics are hard to maintain ``over the net''. 
For example, trusting
user id 0 (root) is not always a good idea.
.IP 2)
Some \*(Un execution semantics are difficult. 
For example, \*(Un
allows a user to remove an open, yet the file does
not disappear until closed by everyone. 
In a network environment a client \*(Un
machine may not own an open file. 
Therefore, a server may remove a client's open file.
.IP 3)
When a \*(Un
machine crashes, it takes all its applications down with it. 
When a
network node crashes (whether client or server), it should not drag all
of its bound neighbors down. 
The treatment of node failure on a network
raises difficulties in any system and is especially difficult in the \*(Un
environment. 
Sun has implemented a system of ``stateless'' protocols to
circumvent the problem of a crashing server dragging down its bound
clients. 
Stateless here means that a client is independently
responsible for completing work, and that a server need not remember
anything from one call to the next. 
In other words, the server keeps
no state. With no state left on the server, there is no state to
recover when the server crashes and comes back up. 
So, from the
client's point of view,
a crashed server appears no different than a very slow server.
.LP
In implementing \*(Un
over the network, Sun attempted to remain compatible with \*(Un
whenever possible. 
However, certain incompatibilities have been
introduced; they are typically of two kinds. 
First those issues that would make a networked \*(Un
evolve into a distributed operating system, rather than a collection
of network services. 
And second, those issues that would make crash
recovery extremely difficult from both the implementation and
administration point of view.
.LP
All incompatibilities are documented in the appropriate sections of
this administration manual.
.bp
.NH 2
A Hint About Debugging \s-2UNIX\s0 In The Network Environment
.LP
When you cannot get something done that involves Sun network services,
the problem probably lies in one of the following four areas. 
They are listed here with the most likely problem first.
.IP 1)
The Sun network access control policies do not allow the
operation, or architectural constraints prevent the operation.
.IP 2)
The client software or environment is broken.
.IP 3)
The server software or environment is broken.
.IP 4)
The network is broken.
.LP
The following sections present specific instructions on how to check
for these causes of failure in the \s-2NFS\s0
and \fLyp\fP environments.
.bp
.NH
\s-2NFS\s+2 The Network File System Service
.LP
We begin with an explanation of some \s-2NFS\s0 terms and concepts. 
Then we describe how to create an \s-2NFS\s0
server that exports file systems, and how to
mount and utilize remote file systems. 
Following that a section on debugging the \s-2NFS\s0
explains what to do when problems occur. 
Finally, some cautionary words about incompatibilities 
between \s-2NFS\s0 files and normal \*(Un files.
.NH 2
What Is The \s-2NFS\s+2 Service
.LP
The \s-2NFS\s0 enables users to share file systems over the network. 
A client may mount or unmount file systems from an \s-2NFS\s0
server machine. 
The client always initiates the binding to a server's
file system by using the \fLmount\fP\|(8) command. 
Typically, a client
mounts one or more remote file systems at startup time by placing
lines like these in the file \fL/etc/fstab\fP, which \fLmount\fP reads
when the system comes up:
.LS
titan:/usr2 /usr2 nfs rw,hard 0 0
venus:/usr/man /usr/man nfs rw,hard 0 0
.LF
See \fLfstab\fP\|(5) for a full description of the format.
.LP
Since clients initiate all remote mounts, \s-2NFS\s0
servers keep control over who may mount a file system by limiting
named file systems to desired clients with an entry in
the \fL/etc/exports\fP file. 
For example:
.LS 0
/usr/local                   # export to the world
/usr2      nixon ford reagan # export to only these machines
.LF
is an \fL/etc/exports\fP entry that speaks for itself. 
Note that
pathnames given in \fL/etc/exports\fP must be the mount point of a
local file system. 
See \fLexports\fP\|(5) for a full description of the format.
.NH 2
How The \s-2NFS\s+2 Works
.LP
Two remote programs implement the \s-2NFS\s0
service \(em \fLmountd\fP\|(8)
and \fLnfsd\fP\|(8). 
A client's \fLmount\fP request talks to
\fLmountd\fP which checks the access permission of the client and
returns a pointer to a filesystem. 
After the \fLmount\fP completes,
access to that mount point and below goes through the pointer to the
server's \fLnfsd\fP daemon using \fLrpc\fP\|(4). 
Client kernel file
access requests (delayed-write and read-ahead) are handled by the
\fLbiod\fP\|(8) daemons on the client.
.LP
If this terse explanation doesn't answer all your questions, you can
find more details in \fIThe Network Services Guide\fP. 
However, you will be able to install and administer the \s-2NFS\s0
just by considering the information given here.
.NH 2
How To Become An \s-2NFS\s+2 Server
.LP
An \s-2NFS\s0 server is simply a machine that exports a file 
system or systems. 
Here are the three steps that any machine must follow to enable
it to export a file system.
.bp
.IP 1)
Become super-user and place the mount-point pathname of the file system
you want to export in the file \fL/etc/exports\fP. 
See \fLexports\fP\|(5) for file format details.
For example, to export
\fL/usr/src/mybin\fP, the export file would look like:
.LS
/usr/src/mybin
.LF
Of course, an \s-2NFS\s0
server may only export file systems of its own.
.IP 2)
As we saw above, \fLmountd\fP must be present for a remote mount to
succeed. 
Make sure \fLmountd\fP will be available for an \fLrpc\fP
call by checking the file \fL/etc/servers\fP for this line:
.LS
rpc     udp  /usr/etc/rpc.mountd 100005 1
.LF
If it isn't there add it. 
For details, see \fLservers\fP\|(5).
.IP 3)
Remote mount also needs some number (typically 4) of \fLnfsd\fP\|'s to
execute on \s-2NFS\s0 servers. 
Check \fL/etc/rc.local\fP for lines like these:
.LS
if [ -f /etc/nfsd -a -f /etc/exports ]; then
/etc/nfsd 4 & echo -n ' nfsd'		>/dev/console
.LF
Add these lines, or your own version of them, if the new \s-2NFS\s0
server's \fL/etc/rc.local\fP does not enable \fLnfsd\fP\^'s. 
You can enable \fLnfsd\fP\|'s without rebooting, by typing, 
while super-user:
.LS
# \f(LB/etc/nfsd 4\fP
.LF
.LP
After these steps, the \s-2NFS\s0
server should be able to export the named file system.
Read the next section and try to remote mount.
.NH 2
How To Remote Mount A File System
.LP
You can mount any exported file system onto your machine, as long as
you can reach its server over the network, and you are included in 
its \fL/etc/export\fP list for that file system. 
On the machine where you want to mount the file system, 
become super-user and type the following:
.LS
# \f(LBmount\fP \fIserver_name\fP\^:\fI/file_system /mount_point\fP
.LF
For example, to mount the manual pages from remote machine \fLelvis\fP
on my directory \fL/usr/elvis.man\fP:
.LS
# \f(LBmount elvis:/usr/man /usr/elvis.man\fP
.LF
.LP
To make sure you have mounted a file system, and mounted it where
you expected to, use either \fLdf\fP\|(1) or \fLmount\fP\|(8), without
an argument. 
Each of these displays the currently mounted file systems.
.LP
Typically, you mount frequently used file systems at startup by
placing an entry for them in the file \fL/etc/fstab\fP. 
You can also see \fLfstab\fP\|(5).
.bp
.NH 2
Typical \s-2NFS\s+2 Layout
.LP
To fully explain the layout of the \s-2NFS\s0
on clients, the output from \fLmount\fP commands below shows
the mounted file systems on a server, and on one of its clients \(em
notice where the client file systems are mounted from. 
Following that,
the output from \fLls\fP commands shows the contents of various
directories on the client machine. \fLlenin\fP is a client
of \fLmarx\fP:
.\"	.PL FULL
.sp 2
.LS 8
marx% \f(LBmount\fP
/dev/xy0a on / type 4.2 (rw)
/dev/xy0g on /pub type 4.2 (rw)
/dev/xy0d on /usr type 4.2 (rw)
/dev/xy2g on /usr2 type 4.2 (rw)
/dev/xy2b on /usr/doctools type 4.2 (rw)
.LF
.LP
