.NH 2 
Debugging the Network File System
.LP
Before trying to debug the \s-2NFS\s0 read the section on 
how the \s-2NFS\s0
works and also the man pages for \fLmount\fP\|(8), \fLnfsd\fP\|(8),
\fLbiod\fP\|(8), \fLshowmount\fP\|(8), \fLrpcinfo\fP\|(8),
\fLmountd\fP\|(8), \fLinetd\fP\|(8), \fLfstab\fP\|(5), \fLmtab\fP\|(5)
and \fLexports\fP\|(5). 
You don't have to understand them fully, but
you should be familiar with the names and functions of the various
daemons and database files.
.LP
When tracking down an \s-2NFS\s0
problem keep in mind that, like all network services, there are three
main points of failure: the server, the client, or the network itself.
The debugging strategy outlined below tries to isolate each individual
component to find the one that isn't working.
.LP
For example, let's look
at a sample mount request as made from an \s-2NFS\s0
client machine:
.LS
% \f(LBmount krypton:/usr/src /krypton.src\fP
.LF
and try to understand how it works and how it can fail. 
The example
asks the server machine ``krypton'' to return a file handle
(\fLfhandle\fP) for the directory \fL/usr/src\fP. 
This \fLfhandle\fP is
then passed to the kernel in the \fLnfsmount\fP\|(2) system call. 
The kernel looks up the directory \fL/krypton.src\fP and, if everything is
okay, it ties the \fLfhandle\fP to the directory in a mount record.
From now on all file system requests to that directory and below will
go through the \fLfhandle\fP to the server ``krypton''.
.LP
That's how it should work, but if you are reading this it probably
didn't. 
So, how did it fail. 
First, some general pointers and then we
will list the possible errors, and what might have caused them.
.NH 3 
General Hints
.LP
When there are network or server problems, programs that access hard
mounted remote files will fail differently than those which access soft
mounted remote files. 
Hard mounted remote file systems cause programs
to retry until the server responds again. 
Soft mounted remote file
systems return an error after trying for a while. \fLmount\fP is like
any other program, if the server for a remote file system fails to
respond it will retry the mount request until it succeeds.
A soft mount will try once in the foreground then background itself
and keep trying.
.LP
Once a hard mount succeeds, programs that access hard mounted files
will hang as long as the server fails to respond. 
In this case, \s-2NFS\s0
should print a \fLserver not responding\fP message on the console. 
On a
soft mounted file system programs will get \fLConnection timed out\fP
(ETIMEDOUT) when they access a file whose server is dead.
Unfortunately, many programs in \*(Un
do not check return conditions on file system operations
so you may not see this error message when accessing soft mounted
files. 
Nevertheless, an \s-2NFS\s0
error message should be printed on the console in this case also.
.LP
If a client is having \s-2NFS\s0
trouble, check first to make sure the server is up and running. 
From a client you can type
.LS
% \f(LB/usr/etc/rpcinfo -p\fP \fIserver_name\fP
.LF
.bp
.LP
to see if the server is up at all. 
It should print out a list of
program, version, protocol, and port numbers that resembles:
.LS
[program, version, protocol, port]:
.sp
[100005, 1, 17, 1072]
[100001, 2, 17, 1081]
[100001, 1, 17, 1081]
[100002, 1, 17, 1078]
[100008, 1, 17, 1075]
[100007, 1, 17, 1035]
[100007, 1,  6, 1027]
[100004, 1,  6, 1026]
[100004, 1, 17, 1024]
.LF
If that works you can also use \fLrpcinfo\fP to check if
the \fLmountd\fP server is running:
.LS
% \f(LB/usr/etc/rpcinfo -u\fP \fIserver_name\fP \f(LB100005 1\fP
.LF
This should come back with the response:
.LS
proc 100005 vers 1 ready and waiting
.LF
If these fail you should go login to the server's console and see if
it is okay.
.LP
If the server is alive but your machine can't reach it you should
check the Ethernet connections between your machine and the server
(see \fIEthernet Troubleshooting\fP in the chapter 
on \fICommunications\fP in this manual).
.LP
If the server is okay and the network is okay use \fLps\fP to check
your client daemons. 
You should have a \fLportmap\fP, \fLypbind\fP, and
several \fLbiod\fP daemons running. 
For example:
.LS
% \f(LBps ax\fP
.LF
should give back lines similar to these:
.LS
32 ?  I     1:07 /etc/portmap
38 ?  I     0:42 /etc/ypbind
61 ?  S     0:45  (biod)
62 ?  S     0:36  (biod)
63 ?  S     0:30  (biod)
64 ?  S     0:27  (biod)
.LF
.LP
The four sections below deal with the most common types of failure. 
The first tells what to do if your remote mount fails, the next three talk
about servers not responding once you have file systems mounted.
.bp
.NH 3 
Remote Mount Failed
.LP
This section deals with problems related to mounting.  
If \fLmount\fP
fails for any reason check the sections below for specific details
about what to do. 
They are arranged according to where they occur in
the mounting sequence and are labeled with the error message you are
likely to see.
.LP
\fLmount\fP can get its parameters either from the command line or
from the file \fL/etc/fstab\fP (see \fLmount\fP\|(8)). 
The example
below assumes command line arguments, but the same debugging
techniques work if \fL/etc/fstab\fP is used in the \fLmount -a\fP
command.
.LP
Keep in mind the interaction of the various players in the mount
request. 
If you understand this, the problem descriptions
below will make a lot more sense.
.LP
Let's look again at the example \fLmount\fP request given above,
.LS
% \f(LBmount krypton:/usr/src /krypton.src\fP
.LF
and see what steps \fLmount\fP goes through to mount a remote file system.
.IP 1) 5
\fLmount\fP opens \fL/etc/mtab\fP and checks that this mount has not
already been done.
.IP 2)
\fLmount\fP parses the first argument into host ``krypton'' and remote
directory ``/usr/src''.
.IP 3)
\fLmount\fP calls the yellow pages binder daemon \fLypbind\fP to
determine which server machine to find the yellow pages server on.  
It then calls the \fLypserv\fP daemon
on that machine to get the internet protocol (IP) address
of krypton.
.IP 4)
\fLmount\fP calls krypton's portmapper to get the port number of
\fLmountd\fP.
.IP 5)
\fLmount\fP calls krypton's \fLmountd\fP and passes it ``/usr/src''.
.IP 6)
Krypton's \fLmountd\fP reads \fL/etc/exports\fP and looks for the
exported file system that contains ``/usr/src''.
.IP 7)
Krypton's \fLmountd\fP calls the yellow pages server \fLypserv\fP to
expand the host names and netgroups in the export list
for ``/usr/src''.
.IP 8)
Krypton's \fLmountd\fP does a \fLgetfh\fP\|(2) system call
on ``/usr/src'' to get the \fLfhandle\fP.
.IP 9)
Krypton's \fLmountd\fP returns the \fLfhandle\fP.
.IP 10)
\fLmount\fP does an \fLnfsmount\fP\|(2) system call with the \fLfhandle\fP
and ``/krypton.src''.
.IP 11)
\fLnfsmount\fP checks if the caller is superuser and
if ``/krypton.src'' is a directory.
.IP 12)
\fLnfsmount\fP does a \fLstatfs\fP\|(2) call to krypton's \s-2NFS\s0
server (\fLnfsd\fP\|).
.IP 13)
\fLmount\fP opens \fL/etc/mtab\fP and adds an entry to the end.
.LP
Any one of these steps can fail, some of them in more than one way.
The sections below give detailed descriptions of the failures
associated with specific error messages.
.KS
.LP 
\fL/etc/mtab: No such file or directory\fP
.IP
The mounted file system table is kept in the file \fL/etc/mtab\fP\|(5).
This file must exist before mount can succeed.
.sp
.KE
.bp
.KS
.LP
\fLmount: ... already mounted\fP
.IP
The file system that you are trying to mount is already mounted or
there is a bogus entry for it in \fL/etc/mtab\fP.
.sp
.KE
.KS
.LP
\fLmount: ... Block device required\fP
.IP
You probably left off the ``krypton:'' part off
.LS
# \f(LBmount krypton:/usr/src /krypton.src\fP
.LF
.IP
The \fLmount\fP command assumes you are doing a local mount unless it
sees a colon in the file system name or the file system type is ``nfs''
in \fL/etc/fstab\fP.
.sp
.KE
.KS
.LP
\fLmount: ... not found in /etc/fstab\fP
.IP
If \fLmount\fP is called with only a directory or file system name but
not both it looks in \fL/etc/fstab\fP for an entry whose file system or
directory field matched the argument. 
For example,
.LS
# \f(LBmount /krypton.src\fP
.LF
.IP
will search \fL/etc/fstab\fP for a line that has a directory name
field of ``/krypton.src''. 
If it finds an entry, such as:
.LS
krypton:/usr/src /krypton.src nfs rw,hard 0 0
.LF
.IP
it will do the mount as if you had typed
.LS
# \f(LBmount krypton:/usr/src /krypton.src\fP
.LF
.IP
If you see this message it means the argument you gave \fLmount\fP was
not in any of the entries in \fL/etc/fstab\fP.
.sp
.KE
.KS
.LP
\fL/etc/fstab: No such file or directory\fP
.IP
\fLmount\fP tried to look up the name in \fL/etc/fstab\fP
but there was no \fL/etc/fstab\fP.
.sp
.KE
.KS
.LP
\fL \.\.\. not in hosts database\fP
.IP
This means the yellow pages could not find the hostname you gave it in
the \fL/etc/hosts\fP database or that the yellow pages daemon
(\fLypbind\fP) is dead on your machine. 
First check the spelling and
the placement of the colon in your mount call. 
If it looks okay make
sure that \fLypbind\fP is running by typing:
.LS
# \f(LBps ax\fP
.LF
.IP
Try \fLrlogin\fP or \fLrcp\fP to some other machine. 
If this also fails
your \fLypbind\fP is probably dead or hung.  
If you only get this
message for one host name it means that the \fL/etc/hosts\fP entry on
the yellow pages server needs to be checked. 
See the section on
debugging the yellow pages below in this chapter.
.KE
.KS
.LP
\fLmount: directory path must begin with '/'\fP
.IP
The second argument to mount is the path of the directory to be
covered.  
This must be an absolute path starting at ``\f(LB/\fP''.
.KE
.KS
.LP
\fLmount: ... server not responding: \s-2RPC_PMAP_FAILURE - RPC_TIMED_OUT\s+2\fP
.IP
Either the server you are trying to mount from is down, or its
portmapper is dead or hung. 
Try logging in to that machine. 
If you can log in, try running:
.LS
# \f(LBrpcinfo -p\fP \fIhostname\fP
.LF
.IP
You should get a list of registered program numbers. 
If you don't, you
should restart the portmapper.  
Note that restarting the portmapper requires that you then kill 
and restart both \fLinetd\fP and \fLypbind\fP. 
To do this, become root and do a
.LS
# \f(LBps ax\fP
.LF
.IP
to find the process ids of \fLportmap\fP, \fLypbind\fP, 
and \fLinetd\fP. 
Next, do a
.LS
# \f(LBkill -9\fP \fIportmap_pid ypbind_pid inetd_pid\fP
.LF
.IP
to kill the daemons.  
Then type
.LS
# \f(LB/etc/portmap\fP
# \f(LB/etc/inetd\fP
# \f(LB/etc/ypbind\fP
.LF
.IP
to start new ones.
.IP
If you don't want to mess around with this, you can just reboot the
server.
.IP
If you can't \fLrlogin\fP to the server but the server is up, you
should check your Ethernet connection by trying \fLrlogin\fP to some
other machine. 
You should also check the server's Ethernet connection.
.sp
.KE
.KS
.LP
\fLmount: ... server not responding: \s-2RPC_PROG_NOT_REGISTERED\s+2\fP
.IP
This means that mount got through to the portmapper but the \s-2NFS\s0
mount daemon (\fLrpc.mountd\fP) was not registered.  
Go to the server
and be sure that \fL/usr/etc/rpc.mountd\fP exists and that there is an
entry in \fL/etc/servers\fP exactly like:
.LS
rpc	udp	/usr/etc/rpc.mountd 100005 1
.LF
.IP
Finally, use \fLps\fP to be sure that the internet daemon (\fLinetd\fP)
is running.  
If you had to change \fL/etc/servers\fP you will have to
kill \fLinetd\fP and restart it.  
Look in \fL/etc/rc.local\fP to see
how it is started at boot time and do that by hand.
.KE
.bp
.KS
.LP
\fLmount: ...: No such file or directory\fP
.IP
Either the remote directory or the local directory doesn't exist.
Check spelling. 
Try to \fLls\fP both directories.
.LP
\fLmount: not in export list for ...\fP
.IP
Your machine name is not in the export list for the file system you
want to mount from the server.
You can get a list of the server's exported file systems by running
.LS
# \f(LBshowmount -e\fP \fIhostname\fP
.LF
.IP
If the file system you want is not in the list, or your machine name
or netgroup name is not in the user list for the file system, login to
the server and check the \fL/etc/exports\fP file for the correct
file system entry. 
A file system name that appears in the
\fL/etc/exports\fP file but not in the output from \fLshowmount\fP,
indicates a failure in \fLmountd\fP. 
Either it could not parse that
line in the file, or it could not find the file system, or the
filesystem name was not a local mounted file system.
See \fLexports\fP\|(5) for more information. 
If \fLexports\fP\ seems
okay check the server's \fLypbind\fP daemon, it may be dead or hung.
.KE
.KS
.LP
\fLmount: ...: Permission denied\fP
.IP
This message is sort of a generic indication that some authentication
failed on the server. 
It could simply be that you are not in the export
list (see above) or the server couldn't figure out who you are
(\fLypbind\fP dead), or it could be that the server doesn't believe you
are who you say you are. 
Check the server's \fL/etc/exports\fP and
\fLypbind\fP. 
In this case you can just change your hostname
(\fLhostname\fP\|(1)) and retry the \fLmount\fP.
.KE
.KS
.LP
\fLmount: ...: Not a directory\fP
.IP
Either the remote path or the local path is not a directory. 
Check spelling and try to \fLls\fP both directories.
.KE
.KS
.LP
\fLmount: ...: Not owner\fP
.IP
You have to do the mount as root on your machine because it affects
the file system for the whole machine, not just you.
.KE
.NH 3 
Programs Hung
.LP
If programs hang doing file related work, your \s-2NFS\s0
server may be dead. 
You may see the message \fLNFS server not responding, 
still trying\fP on your console. 
This is probably a problem either with one of your \s-2NFS\s0
servers or with the Ethernet. 
Programs can also hang if an \fLnd\fP
server dies (see the section on \fLnd\fP above) or if a \fLyp\fP
server dies (see \fLyp\fP below).
.LP
If your machine hangs completely, check the server(s) from which you
have mounted. 
If one of them (or more) is down don't worry, when the
server comes back up your programs will continue automatically and they
won't even know the server died. 
No files will be destroyed.
.LP
If a soft mounted server dies, other work should not be affected.
Programs that time-out trying to
access soft mounted remote files will fail with \fLerrno ETIMEDOUT\fP,
but you should still be able to get work done on your other file
systems.
.bp
.LP
If all of the servers are running go ask someone else using the
server or servers that you are using if they are having trouble.
If more than one machine is having problems getting service, then it
is probably a problem with the server's \s-2NFS\s0
daemon (\fLnfsd\fP\|(4)).
Log in to the server and do a \fLps\fP to see if \fLnfsd\fP is running
and accumulating cpu time.  
If not you may be able to kill and then restart \fLnfsd\fP.  
If this doesn't work you will have to reboot the server.
.LP
If other people seem to be okay you should check your Ethernet
connection and the connection of the server.
.NH 3 
Hangs Part Way Through Boot
.LP
If your machine comes part way up after a boot, but hangs where it
would normally be doing remote mounts, probably one or more
servers is down or your network connection is bad.  
See \fIProgram Hung\fP and \fIRemote Mount Failed\fP above.
.NH 3 
Everything Works But Slowly
.LP
If access to remote files seems unusually slow, type:
.LS
# \f(LBps aux\fP
.LF
on the server to be sure it is not being clobbered by a runaway daemon,
bad \fLtty\fP line, etc. 
If the server seems okay and other people are
getting good response, make sure your block I/O daemons are running;
type \fLps ax\fP and look for \fLbiod\fP. 
If they are not running or are hung you can kill them off by typing
.LS
# \f(LBps ax | grep biod\fP
.LF
to find the process ids, and
.LS
# \f(LBkill -9\fP \fIpid1 pid2 pid3 pid4\fP
.LF
Restart them with
.LS
# \f(LB/etc/biod 4\fP
.LF
To determine whether they are hung, do a \fLps\fP as above, then
copy a large remote file, then do another \fLps\fP. 
If the
\fLbiod\fP\|s don't accumulate cpu time they are probably hung.
.LP
If \fLbiod\fP is okay check your Ethernet connection. 
The
command \fLnetstat -i\fP will tell you if you are dropping packets.
Also, \fLnfsstat -c\fP and \fLnfsstat -s\fP can be used to tell if
the client or server is doing lots of retransmitting.  
A retransmission rate of 5% is considered high. 
Excessive retransmission
usually indicates a bad Ethernet board, a bad Ethernet tap, a mismatch
between board and tap, or a mismatch between your Ethernet board and
the server's board.  
(See \fIEthernet Troubleshooting\fP in the
\fICommunications\fP chapter of this manual).
.bp
.NH 2 
Architectural Incompatibilities To Earlier \s-2UNIX\s+2 Versions
.LP
A few things work differently, or don't work at all, on remote \s-2NFS\s0
file systems. 
Here we discuss the incompatibilities and suggest how
to work around them.
.NH 3 
No Superuser Access Over The Network
.LP
Under the \s-2NFS\s0
a server exports file systems it owns so clients may
remote mount them. 
When a client becomes superuser, it is denied
permission on remote mounted file systems. 
Let's look at the following example:
.LS
% \f(LBcd\fP
% \f(LBtouch test1 test2\fP
% \f(LBchmod 777 test1\fP
% \f(LBchmod 700 test2\fP
% \f(LBls -l test*\fP
-rwxrwxrwx  1 jsbach       0 Mar 24 16:12 test1
-rwx------  1 jsbach       0 Mar 24 16:12 test2
.LF
Now, retry it again as root.
.LS
% \f(LBsu\fP
Password:
# \f(LBtouch test1\fP
# \f(LBtouch test2\fP
touch: test2: Permission denied
# \f(LBls -l test*\fP
-rwxrwxrwx  1 jsbach       0 Mar 21 16:16 test1
-rwx------  1 jsbach       0 Mar 21 16:12 test2
.LF
The problem usually shows up during the execution of a set-uid root
program. 
Programs that run as root cannot access files or directories
unless the permission for ``other'' allows it.
.LP
There's another wrinkle to the problem. 
You cannot change
ownership of remote mounted files. 
Since users cannot do a \fLchown\fP
command and root is treated as a normal user on remote access, no one
but root on the server can change the ownership of remote files.
For example, as yourself, you attempt to \fLchown\fP a new
program, \fLa.out\fP, which must be set-uid root. 
It will not work, as demonstrated here:
.LS
% \f(LBchmod 4777 a.out\fP
% \f(LBsu\fP
Password:
# \f(LBchown root a.out\fP
a.out: Not owner
.LF
To change the ownership, you must login to the server as root, then
make the change. 
Or, you can move the file to a file system owned
by your machine (for example \fL/usr/tmp\fP is always owned by the
local machine) and make the change there.
.LP
In a very friendly network environment, you may choose to
allow root access over the network. 
(Sun does not recommend
over-the-net root access!) 
You may \fLadb\fP\|(1) the server's kernel
in the following way. 
(NOTE: You never change the client's kernel in
this procedure, only the server's.)
.bp
.IP \(bu 4
On the \s-2NFS\s0
server change the value of the kernel variable ``nobody''.
On a running system you type:
.LS
# \f(LBadb -w /vmunix /dev/kmem\fP
.LF
.IP
\fLadb\fP responds:
.LS
not core file = /dev/kmem
.LF
.IP
This is normal. 
Then you type:
.LS
\f(LBnobody/D\fP
.LF
.IP
and \fLadb\fP responds with:
.LS
_nobody:
_nobody:        -2
.LF
.IP
If it does not, stop and call Sun Tech Support for further help.
If it does, then you enter:
.LS
\f(LBnobody/W0\fP
.LF
.IP
and \fLadb\fP responds with:
.LS
_nobody:        -2              =       0
.LF
.IP
(Note: the last character in each of the two previous lines is zero.)
Then you type \fL<ctrl-D>\fP to exit \fLadb\fP. 
The kernel currently running will now allow root access 
to \s-2NFS\s0 clients.
.IP \(bu
If you want to do this on a binary image, for example \fL/vmunix\fP,
then you type:
.LS
# \f(LBadb -w /vmunix\fP
.LF
.IP
\fLadb\fP makes no response, so you type:
.LS
\f(LBnobody?D\fP
.LF
.IP
and \fLadb\fP responds:
.LS
_nobody:
_nobody:        -2
.LF
.IP
Again, if \fLadb\fP does not say this, stop and call Sun Tech Support
for further help. 
You then type:
.LS
\f(LBnobody?W0\fP
.LF
.IP
and \fLadb\fP responds:
.LS
_nobody:        -2              =       0
.LF
.IP
(Note: the last character in each of the two previous lines is zero.)
Then you type \fL<ctrl-D>\fP to exit \fLadb\fP.
From now on, every time you boot \fL/vmunix\fP, the system
will allow root access across the \s-2NFS\s0
.bp
.IP \(bu
If you want to make new kernels that allow root access,
you can either go through the procedure above for a binary image
each time, or you can go the directory where the kernel
object files are and type:
.LS
# \f(LBadb -w nfs_server.o\fP
.LF
.IP
You continue as above for in the binary image example. 
Then every kernel you make with these object files will allow 
root access on the \s-2NFS\s0.
.NH 3 
File Operations Not Supported
.LP
File locking is not supported on remote file systems. 
Therefore,
the \fLflock\fP\|(2) call will fail when locking a remote file.
.LP
In addition append mode and atomic writes are not guaranteed to work on
remote files accessed by more than one client simultaneously.
.NH 3 
Cannot Access Remote Devices
.LP
In the \s-2NFS\s0
you cannot access a remote mounted device or any other
character or block special file \(em like named pipes.
.NH 2 
Clock Skew In User Programs
.LP
Since the \s-2NFS\s0
architecture differs is some minor ways from earlier versions of \*(Un,
you should be mindful of those places where your own programs could
run up against these incompatibilities. Be sure to read the section
above, \fIArchitectural Incompatibilities\fP, for a discussion of what
will not work over the network.
.LP
Because each workstation keeps its own time, the clocks will be out of
sync between the \s-2NFS\s0
server and client. 
Obviously this might introduce
a problem in certain situations. 
In the 2.0 release, we have fixed all
the clock skew problems we have seen. 
Here are examples of two problems and how they were fixed.
.IP 1) 4
Many programs make the (reasonable) assumption that an existing file
could not have been created in the future. 
For example \fLls\fP does it. 
The command \fLls -l\fP has two basic forms of output, depending
upon how old the file is:
.LS
# \f(LBdate\fP
Jan 22 15:27:01 PST 1985
# \f(LBtouch file2\fP
# \f(LBls -l file*\fP
-rw-r--r--  1 root          0 Dec 27  1983 file
-rw-r--r--  1 root          0 Jan 22 15:27 file2
.LF
The first form of \fLls\fP prints the year, month, and day of last file
modification if the file is more than six months old.
The second form prints the month, day, and minute of last file
modification if the file is less than six months old.
.IP
\fLls\fP calculates the age of a file by simply subtracting the
modification time of the file from the current time. 
If the results are
greater than six months worth of seconds, the file is ``old''.
.bp
.IP
Now assume that the time on the server is Jan 22 15:30:31
(three minutes ahead of our local machine's time):
.LS
# \f(LBdate\fP
Jan 22 15:27:31 PST 1985
# \f(LBtouch file3\fP
# \f(LBls -l file*\fP
-rw-r--r--  1 root          0 Dec 27  1983 file
-rw-r--r--  1 root          0 Jan 22 15:26 file2
-rw-r--r--  1 root          0 Jan 22  1985 file3
.LF
.IP
The problem is that the difference of the two times is huge:
.DS
(now) - (modification_time) =
(now) - (now + 180 seconds) =
-180 seconds = huge unsigned number,
which is greater than six months.
.DE
.IP
Thus, \fLls\fP believes the new file was created long ago in the past.
Sun modified \fLls\fP to deal with files which are created a short
time in the future.
.IP 2)
\fLranlib\fP\|(1) was also modified to deal with clock skew.
\fLranlib\fP timestamps a library when it is produced, and \fLld\fP
compares that timestamp with the last modified date of the library.
If the last modified date occurred after the timestamp, then \fLld\fP
instructs the user to run \fLranlib\fP again, and reload the library.
.IP
If the library lives on a server whose clock is ahead of the client
that runs \fLranlib\fP, \fLld\fP will always complain.
Sun fixed \fLranlib\fP to set the timestamp to the maximum value of
the current time and the library's modify time.
.LP
In general remember, if your application depends upon local time and/or
the file system timestamps, then it will have to deal with
clock skew problems if it uses remote files.
.bp
.NH
The Sun Yellow Pages Service
.LP
We begin by introducing \fLyp\fP related terms. 
Following that we list
the new \fLyp\fP manual pages; that section also includes pointers to
\fLyp\fP documentation beyond this chapter. 
Next, you will find
explanations of the installation and administration of \fLyp\fP. 
Then a section on how to debug \fLyp\fP when problems occur. 
And finally,
we discuss file access policies and special security issues raised by
the \fLyp\fP environment.
.NH 2 
What Is The Yellow Pages Service?
.LP
The yellow pages is Sun's distributed network lookup service:
.IP \(bu
\fLyp\fP is a distributed system, the database is fully replicated at
several sites, each of which runs a server process for the database.
These sites are known as \fLyp\fP servers. 
The multiple servers
propagate updated databases among themselves to ensure database
consistency. 
At steady state, it doesn't matter which server process
answers a client request, the answer will be the same all over.
This allows multiple servers per network, giving the \fLyp\fP
service a high degree of availability and reliability.
.IP \(bu
\fLyp\fP is a lookup service. 
It maintains a set of databases which may be queried. 
A client may ask for the value associated
with a particular key within a database, and may enumerate every
key-value pair within a database.
.IP \(bu
\fLyp\fP is a network service. 
It uses a standard set of access
procedures to hide the details of where and how data is stored.
.NH 3 
The \fLyp\fP Map
.LP
The \fLyp\fP system serves information stored in \fLyp\fP maps.
Each map contains a set of keys and associated values.
For example, in a map called \fLhosts\fP, all the host names within a
network are the keys, and the internet addresses of these host names
are the values. 
Each \fLyp\fP map has a \fLmapname\fP used by programs
to access it. 
Programs must know the format of the data in the map. 
Many of the current maps are derived
from ASCII files traditionally found in \fL/etc\fP\|:  \fLhosts\fP,
\fLgroup\fP, \fLpasswd\fP, and a few others. 
The format of the data
within the \fLyp\fP map is identical (in most cases) to the format
within the ASCII file. 
Maps are implemented by \fLdbm\fP\|(3) files
located in the subdirectories of the directory
\fL/etc/yp/\fP\fIyour_domain\fP on \fLyp\fP server machines.
.NH 3 
The \fLyp\fP Domain
.LP
A \fLyp\fP domain is a named set of \fLyp\fP maps. 
You can determine
and set your \fLyp\fP domain with the \fLdomainname\fP\|(1) command.
Note that \fLyp\fP domains are different from both Internet domains and
\fLsendmail\fP domains. 
A \fLyp\fP domain is simply a directory in
\fL/etc/yp\fP containing a set of maps.
A \fLyp\fP server holds all of the maps of a \fLyp\fP domain in a
subdirectory of \fL/etc/yp\fP. 
The name of the subdirectory is the name of the domain. 
For example, maps for the \fLliterature\fP domain would
be in \fL/etc/yp/literature\fP. 
Every \fLyp\fP server serves at
least one domain: ``yp_private'', which \fLyp\fP uses to
find out about other useful domains particular to each site \(em see
\fLypfiles\fP\|(8).
.LP
A domain name is required for retrieving data from a \fLyp\fP database.
Each machine on the network belongs to a default domain
set at boot time in \fL/etc/rc.local\fP with the \fLdomainname\fP\|(1)
command. 
A domain name must be set on all machines, both servers
and clients. 
Further, a single name should be used on all machines on
a network.
.NH 3 
Masters And Slaves
.LP
In the yellow pages environment only a few machines have a set of
\fLyp\fP databases. 
The \fLyp\fP service makes the database set available
over the network. 
A \fLyp\fP client machine runs \fLyp\fP processes and
requests data from databases on other machines. 
Two kinds of machines
have databases: a \fLyp\fP slave server and a \fLyp\fP master server.
The master server updates the databases of the slave servers.
Make changes to databases only on the \fLyp\fP master server.
The changes will propagate from the master server to the \fLyp\fP slave
servers. 
If \fLyp\fP databases are created or changed on slave server
machines instead of master server machines, the \fLyp\fP's update
algorithm will get broken. 
Always do all the database creation and
modification on the master server machine.
.LP
In theory, a server may be a master with regard to one
map, and a slave with regard to another. 
Random assignment of maps
to master or slave machines could introduce a great deal of confusion.
We strongly urge you to make a single server the master for all the
maps created by \fLypinit\fP\|(8) within a single domain. 
This document
assumes that one server is the master for all maps in the database.
.NH 2 
Yellow Pages Overview
.LP
The yellow pages can serve up any number of databases. 
Typically these
include some files that used to be found in \fL/etc\fP. 
For example,
before Sun release 2.0 programs would read the \fL/etc/hosts\fP
file to find an Internet address. 
When a new machine was added to the network, a new
entry had to be added to every machine's \fL/etc/hosts\fP file. 
With the yellow pages, programs that want to look at 
the \fL/etc/hosts\fP file now do a remote procedure 
call (\fLrpc\fP\|) to the servers to get the information.
.LP
Most of the information describing the structure of the \fLyp\fP system
and the commands available for that system is contained in manual
pages, and is not repeated here. 
For quick reference, we list
the man pages and an abstract of their contents here.
For more information, see the \fINetwork Services Guide\fP.
.LP
.RS
\fLypserv\fP\|(8) \(em describes the processes which comprise the
\fLyp\fP system. 
These are \fLypserv\fP, the \fLyp\fP database server
daemon, and \fLypbind\fP, the \fLyp\fP binder daemon.  \fLypserv\fP
must run on each \fLyp\fP server machine. \fLypbind\fP must run on all
machines which use \fLyp\fP services, both servers and clients.
.LP
\fLypfiles\fP\|(8) \(em describes the database structure of the
\fLyp\fP system. 
The domain ``yp_private'' and those maps
used by the \fLyp\fP system itself are described.
.bp
.LP
\fLypinit\fP\|(8) \(em many maps must be constructed from files located
in \fL/etc\fP, such as \fL/etc/hosts\fP, \fL/etc/passwd\fP and others.
The database initialization tool \fLypinit\fP\|(8) does all such
construction automatically. 
In addition, it constructs initial versions
of maps required by the system but not built from files in
\fL/etc\fP\|; examples are the maps ``ypdomains'', ``ypmaps'',
and ``ypservers''. 
Use this tool to set up the master \fLyp\fP server
and the slave \fLyp\fP servers for the first time. 
Do not (generally)
use it as an administrative tool for running systems.
.LP
\fLypmake\fP\|(8) \(em describes the use of \fL/etc/yp/Makefile\fP,
which builds several commonly-changed components of the \fLyp\fP's
database. 
These are the maps built from several ASCII files normally
found in \fL/etc\fP:  \fLpasswd\fP, \fLhosts\fP, \fLgroup\fP,
\fLnetgroup\fP, \fLnetworks\fP, \fLprotocols\fP, and \fLservices\fP.
.LP
\fLmakedbm\fP\|(8) \(em describes a low-level tool for building 
a \fLdbm\fP file which is a valid \fLyp\fP map. 
Databases not
built from \fL/etc/yp/Makefile\fP may be built or rebuilt using
\fLmakedbm\fP. \fLmakedbm\fP may also be used to ``disassemble'' a map
so that you can see the key-value pairs which comprise it. 
The disassembled form may also be modified with standard tools (such as
editors, \fLawk\fP, \fLgrep\fP, and \fLcat\fP), and is in the form
required for input back into \fLmakedbm\fP.
.LP
\fLyppush\fP\|(8) \(em describes a set of tools to administer a running
\fLyp\fP system. \fLyppush\fP tells a master \fLyp\fP server to notice
a new version of a map. 
The new map must be one which has an entry in
the map ``ypmaps'' for the domain, and of which the server is the
master, according to ``ypmaps''. 
After noticing the new version of the
map, the master \fLyp\fP server will tell all its peers within a domain
that it is the master of that map, and to get a new versions from it
(the master).
.LP
\fLyppull\fP\|(8) \(em tells a slave \fLyp\fP server to try to get
a new copy of some map.
.LP
\fLyppoll\fP\|(8) \(em asks any \fLypserv\fP for the information
it holds internally about a single map.
.LP
\fLypcat\fP\|(1) \(em dumps out the contents of a \fLyp\fP map. 
Use it when you don't care which server's version you are seeing. 
If you need
to see a particular server's map, \fLrlogin\fP to that server (or use
\fLrsh\fP) and use \fLmakedbm\fP.
.LP
\fLypwhich\fP\|(8) \(em tells you which \fLyp\fP server a node is using
at the moment for \fLyp\fP services.  
.RE
.NH 2 
Yellow Pages Installation and Administration
.LP
Nine installation and administration topics will be covered:
.RS
.nf
1.)  setting up a master \fLyp\fP server
2.)  altering a \fLyp\fP client's database to use \fLyp\fP services
3.)  setting up a slave \fLyp\fP server
4.)  setting up a \fLyp\fP client
5.)  modifying individual \fLyp\fP maps after \fLyp\fP installation
6.)  making new \fLyp\fP maps after \fLyp\fP installation
7.)  propagating new \fLyp\fP maps
8.)  adding a new \fLyp\fP server not in the original set of \fLyp\fP servers
9.)  changing the master server to a different machine
.fi
.RE
.bp
.NH 3 
How To Set Up A Master \fLyp\fP Server
.LP
To create a new master server, become superuser and change your current
directory to \fL/etc/yp\fP. 
Then run \fLypinit\fP\|(8) with
the \fL-m\fP switch. 
The default domain name and the hostname must be
set up; the usual case is that they have been set up from
\fL/etc/rc.local\fP. 
You will be asked whether you want the procedure
to die at the first non-fatal error (in which case, you can fix the
problem and restart \fLypinit\fP \(em this is recommended if you
haven't done the procedure before), or to continue despite non-fatal
errors. 
In this second case you can try to fix all the problems by
hand, or fix some, then restart \fLypinit\fP. \fLypinit\fP will prompt
you for a list of other hosts which also will be \fLyp\fP servers.
(Initially, this will be the set of \fLyp\fP slave servers, but at some
future time any of them might become the \fLyp\fP master
server.) 
You need not add any other hosts at this time, but if you know
that you will be setting up some more \fLyp\fP servers, add them now.
You will save yourself some work later if you do, and there is very
little runtime penalty for doing it. 
(There is \fIsome\fP, however, so don't name every host in the net.)
.LP
Prior to running \fLypinit\fP, the following files in \fL/etc\fP
should be complete and reflect an up-to-date picture of your system:
\fLpasswd\fP, \fLhosts\fP, \fLgroup\fP, \fLnetworks\fP,
\fLprotocols\fP, and \fLservices\fP. 
In addition, if you know how
\fL/etc/netgroup\fP is going to be set up, do that prior to
\fLypinit\fP. 
If you don't know, \fLypinit\fP will make an empty ``netgroup'' map.
.LP
For security reasons you may restrict access to the master \fLyp\fP
machine to a smaller set of users than that defined by the complete
\fL/etc/passwd\fP. 
To do this, copy the complete file to someplace
other than \fL/etc/passwd\fP, and edit out undesired users from the
remaining \fL/etc/passwd\fP. 
For a security-conscious system, this
smaller file should not include the \fLyp\fP escape entry discussed
in the next section.
.LP
To start providing yellow pages services, invoke \fL/etc/ypserv\fP.
It will be started up automatically from \fL/etc/rc.local\fP on
subsequent reboots.
.NH 3 
How To Alter A \fLyp\fP Client's File Database So That \fLyp\fP Services Get Used
.LP
Once the decision has been made to serve a database with the \fLyp\fP,
it is desirable that all nodes in the net access the \fLyp\fP's
version of the information, rather than
the potentially out-of-date information in their local files. 
That policy is enforced by running a \fLypbind\fP process on the client node
(including nodes which may be running \fLyp\fP servers), and by
abbreviating or eliminating the files which traditionally implemented
the database. 
The files in question are: \fL/etc/passwd\fP,
\fL/etc/hosts\fP, \fL/etc/group\fP, \fL/etc/networks\fP,
\fL/etc/protocols\fP, \fL/etc/services\fP, \fL/etc/netgroup\fP,
\fL/etc/hosts.equiv\fP, and \fL/.rhosts\fP. 
The treatment of each file is discussed in this section.
.bp
.IP \(bu
\fL/etc/networks\fP, \fL/etc/protocols\fP,
\fL/etc/services\fP, and \fL/etc/netgroup\fP need not exist at any
\fLyp\fP client node. 
If you are squeamish about removing them, move them to 
backup names; for instance on a machine named ``ypclient'':
.LS
ypclient%  \f(LBcd /etc\fP
ypclient%  \f(LBmv networks networks-\fP
ypclient%  <The rest of the renames, similarly>
.LF
.IP \(bu
\fL/etc/hosts.equiv\fP is not normally served by the \fLyp\fP. 
However, you can add escape sequences to activate the \fLyp\fP. 
This reduces problems with \fLrlogin\fP, or \fLrsh\fP
which are sometimes caused by different \fL/etc/hosts.equiv\fP files on the
two machines.
.IP
To let anyone log on to a machine,
\fL/etc/hosts.equiv\fP may be edited to contain a single line,
with only the character `\fL+\fP' (plus) on it. 
A line with only a ``\fL+\fP'' means that all further entries 
will be retrieved from the \fLyp\fP rather than the local file.
.IP
Alternatively, more control may be exercised over logins by using lines
of the form:
.LS
+@trusted_group1
+@trusted_group2
-@distrusted_group
.LF
Each of the names to the right of the ``\fL@\fP'' (at) character is
assumed to be a group name, defined in the global netgroup database.
The netgroup database is served by the \fLyp\fP.
.IP
If none of the escape sequences is used, only the entries in
\fL/etc/hosts.equiv\fP is used, the \fLyp\fP is not used.
.IP \(bu
\fL/.rhosts\fP is not normally served by the \fLyp\fP, either. 
Its format is identical to that of \fL/etc/hosts.equiv\fP. 
However, since
this file controls remote root access to the local machine,
unrestricted access is not recommended. 
Make the list of trusted hosts
explicit, or use group names for the same purpose.
.IP \(bu
\fL/etc/hosts\fP must contain entries for the local host's name,
and the local loopback name. 
These are accessed at boot time when
the \fLyp\fP service is not yet available.  
After the system is
running, and after the \fLypbind\fP process is up, the \fL/etc/hosts\fP
file is not accessed at all. 
An example of the hosts file for \fLyp\fP client ``zippy'' is:
.LS
127.1		localhost
192.9.1.87	zippy	# John Q. Adams
.LF
.IP \(bu
\fL/etc/passwd\fP should contain entries for root and the primary users
of the machine, and an escape entry to force the use of the \fLyp\fP
service. 
A few additional entries are recommended: \fLdaemon\fP, to
allow file-transfer utilities to work; \fLsync\fP, to run \fLsync\fP on
a screwed-up machine before rebooting; and \fLoperator\fP, to let a
dump operator login. 
A sample \fLyp\fP client's \fL/etc/passwd\fP file looks like:
.bp
.LS
root:wAm0Y4lEnf6:0:10:God:/:/bin/csh
jrandom:uHP1gQ2:1429:10:J Random:/usr2/jrandom:/bin/csh
operator:VyZr6V9:333:20:sys op:/usr2/operator:/bin/csh
daemon:*:1:1::/:
sync::1:1::/:/bin/sync
+::0:0:::
.LF
.IP
The last line informs the library routines to use the \fLyp\fP service
rather than give up the search.  
Entries which exist in \fL/etc/passwd\fP will mask analogous 
entries in the \fLyp\fP maps. 
In addition, earlier entries in the file will mask later ones with the
same user name, or the same uid. 
Therefore, please note the order of
the entries for daemon and for sync (which have the same uid) and
duplicate it in your own file.
.IP \(bu
\fL/etc/group\fP may be reduced to a single line:
.LS
+:
.LF
which will force all translation of group names and group ids to be
made via the \fLyp\fP service. 
This is the recommended procedure.
.NH 3 
How To Set Up A Slave \fLyp\fP Server
.LP
The network must be working to set up a slave \fLyp\fP server \(em in
particular, you must be able to \fLrcp\fP files from the
master \fLyp\fP server to \fLyp\fP slaves.
.LP
To create a new slave server, change directory to \fL/etc/yp\fP. 
From there run \fLypinit\fP\|(8) with the
.B -s
switch, and name a host already set up as a \fLyp\fP server, as the
master. 
Ideally, the named host really is the master server, but it can
be any host which has its \fLyp\fP database set up. 
The \fLypserv\fP
process need not be running on the master host, but the host has to be
reachable. 
You must be superuser when you run \fLypinit\fP. 
The default
domain name on the machine intended to be the \fLyp\fP slave server
must be set up, and must be set to the same domain name as the default
domain name on the machine named as the master. 
In addition, an entry
for ``daemon'' must exist in the \fL/etc/passwd\fP files of both
slave and master, and that entry must precede any other entries which
have the same uid. 
(\fLsetup\fP creates \fL/etc/passwd\fP; make sure
your password file has not been altered to change the order. 
Note the example shown in the section above.) 
You won't be prompted for a list
of other servers, but you will have the opportunity to choose whether
or not the procedure gives up at the first non-fatal error.
.LP
After running \fLypinit\fP, make copies of \fL/etc/passwd\fP,
\fL/etc/hosts\fP, \fL/etc/group\fP, \fL/etc/networks\fP,
\fL/etc/protocols\fP, \fL/etc/netgroup\fP, and \fL/etc/services\fP. 
For instance on a machine named ``ypslave'':
.LS
ypslave% \f(LBcp /etc/passwd /etc/passwd-\fP
.LF
.LP
Edit the original files in accordance with the section above on
altering the client's database, so as to insure that processes on the
slave \fLyp\fP server will actually make use of the \fLyp\fP services,
rather than the local ASCII files. 
(That is, make sure the \fLyp\fP
slave server is also a \fLyp\fP client.)
Make backup copies of the edited files, as well. 
For instance:
.bp
.LS
ypslave% \f(LBcp /etc/passwd /etc/passwd+\fP
.LF
.LP
After the \fLyp\fP database gets set up by \fLypinit\fP, type
\fL/etc/ypserv\fP to begin supplying \fLyp\fP services.
On subsequent reboots, it will start automatically from
\fL/etc/rc.local\fP
.NH 3 
How To Set Up A \fLyp\fP Client
.LP
To set up a \fLyp\fP client, edit the local files as described in the
section above on altering a \fLyp\fP client's file database. 
If \fL/etc/ypbind\fP is not running already, start it.
With the ASCII databases of \fL/etc\fP abbreviated and
\fL/etc/ypbind\fP running, the processes on the machine will be
clients of the \fLyp\fP services. 
At this point, there must be a
\fLyp\fP server available; all sorts of stuff will hang up if no
\fLyp\fP server is available while \fLypbind\fP is running. 
Note the
possible alterations to the client's \fL/etc\fP database as discussed
above in the section on altering the client. 
Because some files may not
be there, or some may be specially altered, it is not always obvious
how the ASCII databases are being used.
The escape conventions used within those files to force inclusion and
exclusion of data from the \fLyp\fP databases are found in the
following man pages: \fLpasswd\fP\|(5), \fLhosts\fP\|(5),
\fLnetgroup\fP\|(5), \fLhost.equiv\fP\|(5), \fLgroup\fP\|(5).
In particular, notice that changing
passwords in \fL/etc/passwd\fP (by editing the file, or by running
\fLpasswd\fP\|(1)), will only affect the local client's environment.
Change \fL/etc/passwd\fP on the master, as described in the next
section, to make a global password change.
.NH 3 
How To Modify Existing \fLyp\fP Maps After \fLyp\fP Installation
.LP
Databases served by the \fLyp\fP must be changed ON THE MASTER SERVER.
The databases expected to change most frequently, like
\fL/etc/passwd\fP, may be changed by first editing the ASCII file,
and then running \fLmake\fP\|(1) on \fL/etc/yp/Makefile\fP \(em
also see \fLypmake\fP\|(8).
.LP
Non-standard databases (that is, databases which are specific to the
applications of a particular vendor or site, but which are not part of
Sun's release), or databases which are expected to change rarely, or
databases for which no ASCII form exists (for example, databases
not around before the \fLyp\fP), may be modified ``manually''. 
The general procedure is to use \fLmakedbm\fP\|(8) with the
.B -u
switch to disassemble them into a form which can be modified using
standard tools (such as \fLawk\fP, \fLsed\fP, or \fLvi\fP\|). 
Then build a new version again using \fLmakedbm\fP\|(8). 
This may be done by hand in two ways:
.IP 1)
The output of \fLmakedbm\fP can be redirected to a temporary file
which can be modified, then fed back into \fLmakedbm\fP, or
.IP 2)
The output of \fLmakedbm\fP can be operated on within a pipeline
which feeds into \fLmakedbm\fP again directly. 
This is appropriate if the disassembled map can be updated by 
modifying it with \fLawk\fP, \fLsed\fP, or a \fIcat\fP append, 
for instance.
.bp
.LP
Suppose we want to create a non-standard \fLyp\fP map, called ``mymap''.
We want it to consist of key-value pairs in which the keys are strings
like al, bl, cl, etc. (the ``l'' is for ``left''), and the values are ar,
br, cr (the ``r'' is for ``right''). 
There are two possible procedures to
follow when creating new maps. 
In one we use an existing ASCII file as
input; in the other we use standard input.
.LP
For example, suppose there is an existing ASCII file
named ``/etc/yp/mymap.asc'', created with an editor or a shell script
on our machine ``ypmaster''. 
\&``home_domain'' is the subdirectory
where the map is located.
We can create the \fLyp\fP map for this file by:
.LS
ypmaster%  \f(LBcd /etc/yp\fP
ypmaster%  \f(LBmakedbm mymap.asc home_domain/mymap\fP
.LF
.LP
But at this point we notice the map really should have
included another 2-tuple: (dl, dr). 
We can make the modification quite simply. 
In all situations like this, remember to modify the map by
first modifying the ASCII file. 
Modifications made to the map, not
also in the ASCII file, will be lost. 
Make the modification like this:
.LS
ypmaster%  \f(LBcd /etc/yp\fP <if not already there>
ypmaster%  <make editorial change to mymap.asc>
ypmaster% \f(LBmakedbm mymap.asc home_domain/mymap\fP
.LF
.LP
When there is no original ASCII file, we can create the \fLyp\fP map
from the keyboard by typing input like this (our machine name
is ``ypmaster'', and the default domain is ``home_domain''):
.LS
ypmaster% \f(LBcd /etc/yp\fP
ypmaster% \f(LBmakedbm - home_domain/mymap\fP
al ar
bl br
cl cr
\f(LB<ctl D>\fP
.LF
.LP
When you need to modify that map, you can use \fLmakedbm\fP to create
a temporary ASCII intermediate file
which can be edited using standard tools. 
For instance:
.LS
ypmaster%  \f(LBcd /etc/yp\fP
ypmaster%  \f(LBmakedbm -u home_domain/mymap > mymap.temp\fP
.LF
.LP
At this point ``mymap.temp'' can be edited to contain the correct
information. 
A new version of the database is created by the commands
.LS
ypmaster%  \f(LBmakedbm mymap.temp home_domain/mymap\fP
ypmaster%  \f(LBrm mymap.temp\fP
.LF
.LP
The preceding paragraphs explained how to use some tools, but in
reality almost everything you actually have to do can be done 
by \fLypinit\fP\|(8) and \fL/etc/yp/Makefile\fP, unless you add
non-standard maps to the database, or change the set of \fLyp\fP
servers after the system is already up and running.
.LP
Whether you use the Makefile in \fL/etc/yp\fP or some other procedure
\(em Makefile is one of many possible \(em the goal is the same: a
new pair of well-formed \fLdbm\fP files must end up in the domain
directory on the master \fLyp\fP server.
.bp
.NH 3 
How To Make New \fLyp\fP Maps After \fLyp\fP Installation
.LP
When you make a new yellow pages map, you must update \fLyp\fP\^'s
database of maps (\fLypmaps\fP\|) to include the new one. 
Suppose your new map is called ``ishmael'', and ``home_domain'' 
is the subdirectory where the maps are kept.
Then, on the \fLyp\fP master server, you would type:
.LS
ypmaster%  \f(LBcd /etc/yp\fP
ypmaster%  \f(LB(makedbm -u home_domain/ypmaps;\e
echo ishmael) | makedbm - tmpmap\fP
.LF
Note that we display the second command above on two lines. 
You may type it in as one long command (even if the line 
wraps on your screen), or you may escape the return and 
newline with a backslash, as shown here. 
However, you cannot simply type in half the command, hit return,
and type the second half.
.LP
\fLmakedbm\fP makes two files \fLtmpmap.dir\fP and \fLtmpmap.pag\fP.
Move those two as shown below:
.LS
ypmaster%  \f(LBmv tmpmap.dir home_domain/ypmaps.dir\fP
ypmaster%  \f(LBmv tmpmap.pag home_domain/ypmaps.pag\fP
ypmaster%  \f(LByppush ypmaps\fP
.LF
.LP
A temporary file must be used here so the second invocation 
of \fLmakedbm\fP doesn't clobber the \fLypmaps\fP before the first
invocation gets to disassemble it.
.LP
Once you have made changes to an existing database or created a new
one, you should edit \fL/etc/yp/Makefile\fP to permanently incorporate
the procedure.
.NH 3 
How To Propagate A New \fLyp\fP Database
.LP
A short time after a database has been newly created or altered,
the \fLyp\fP system will find out about it and will
propagate the changes. 
If you want to hurry things up, you can run
\fLyppush\fP\|(8) to inform the master server that a new version of the
map has been placed in the domain. 
You must update the map
named ``ypmaps'' to include a new map named ``foo'', and then run
\fLyppush\fP for ``ypmaps'' before trying to run \fLyppush\fP
for ``foo''.
.LP
The master will try to tell its slaves about new versions of maps, but
if a slave is overloaded or unreachable, that slave may not find out
about the change. 
Further, the master may be overloaded when the slave tries to 
get the new version from the master. 
This means that \fLyppush\fP may not work. 
You can use \fLyppoll\fP\|(8) to find out which version is where. 
If you
believe that the correct version is present at the master, and that the
master knows about it, but that it is not yet at a slave in which you
have special interest, you may run \fLyppull\fP\|(8) to ``kick'' that
slave into trying to get the map from the master. 
Run \fLyppush\fP
first if you plan on running \fLyppull\fP at all, so that the master
can correctly report his current version to his peers. 
Here again,
if the master is overloaded, the peer may not be able to get the new
version from the master; you can tell by using \fLyppoll\fP.
.bp
.LP
It is very important to note there is a delay between the time you
run \fLyppush\fP or \fLyppull\fP and the time the database transfer
completes. 
Generally it takes a minute or two. 
However, if the map
hasn't been moved within 5 minutes, it's probably not going to make it.
Make sure that both the master and the slave are up, and try again.
.NH 3 
How To Add A New \fLyp\fP Server Not In The Original Set Of \fLyp\fP Servers
.LP
To add a new \fLyp\fP slave server start by modifying some maps on the
master \fLyp\fP server. 
If the new server is a host which has not been a \fLyp\fP server 
before, the host's name must be added to the map ``ypservers'' 
in the domain ``yp_private'' and in the default domain.
The sequence for adding a server named ``ypslave'' to 
domain ``home_domain'' is:
.LS
ypmaster% \f(LBcd /etc/yp\fP
ypmaster% \f(LB(makedbm -u yp_private/ypservers;\e
echo ypslave ypslave)|makedbm - tmpmap\fP
ypmaster% \f(LBmv tmpmap.dir yp_private/ypservers.dir\fP
ypmaster% \f(LBmv tmpmap.pag yp_private/ypservers.pag\fP
ypmaster% \f(LB(makedbm -u home_domain/ypservers;\e
echo ypslave ypslave)|makedbm - tmpmap\fP
ypmaster% \f(LBmv tmpmap.dir home_domain/ypservers.dir\fP
ypmaster% \f(LBmv tmpmap.pag home_domain/ypservers.pag\fP
.LF
.LP
Note that we display some commands above on two lines. 
You may
type these as one long command (even if the line wraps on your screen),
or you may escape the return and newline with a backslash, as shown
here. 
However, you cannot simply type in half the command, hit return,
and type the second half.
.LP
The host's address should be in ``hosts.byname''.
If that's not true, edit \fL/etc/hosts\fP and run \fLmake\fP. In this
case the commands should be:
.LS
ypmaster%  <edit /etc/hosts here>
ypmaster% \f(LBcd /etc/yp\fP
ypmaster% \f(LBmake NOPUSH=1 hosts\fP
ypmaster% \f(LBcp home_domain/hosts.byname.* yp_private\fP
.LF
.LP
After updating the databases, they should be \fLyppush\fPed and
\fLyppull\fPed around as described in the section above on modifying an
individual database. 
The new slave \fLyp\fP server's databases
should be set up by copying the databases from \fLyp\fP master 
server ``ypmaster''. 
Remote login to the new \fLyp\fP slave, and 
use \fLypinit\fP\|(8) in the following way:
.LS
ypslave% \f(LBcd /etc/yp\fP
ypslave% \f(LBypinit -s ypmaster\fP
.LF
Then complete the steps described above in the section \fIHow To Set
Up A Slave yp Server\fP.
.bp
.NH 3 
How To Change The Master Server
.LP
When changing master servers, you will
follow one of two procedures: the first when the master is out
of service; the second when the master is running and will convert
to running as a slave.
.LP
\fBMaster Out Of Service\fP \(em
When the old master is down, begin on an operating slave \fLyp\fP
server. 
The slave must have
a complete set of ASCII files (\fL/etc/passwd\fP, and the others) so
that updates can be done there when it becomes the new master. 
Ideally, these files have been restored from a recent backup of 
the old master, so that recent changes are reflected.
.LP
To change the slave to a master, the map ``ypmaps'' must be changed in
both domain ``yp_private'' and the default domain. 
There is no intermediate ASCII form for this map. 
Probably the easiest way to
modify ``ypmaps'' so that each map mentioned in it points at the new
master is to use \fLmakedbm\fP\|(8) to create a temporary ASCII file,
edit that, and then push the temp file through \fLmakedbm\fP
again to update the maps. 
At this point, if it is running, kill \fLypserv\fP on the slave.  
(Re)start \fLypserv\fP. 
That server now believes that it is the master server. 
Now we have to get the other
\fLyp\fP servers to think so too.  
Run \fLyppush\fP\|(8) for both the
default domain and for ``yp_private'' for the map ``ypmaps'', and also
for ``ypservers'' and/or ``hosts.byname'' and ``hosts.byaddr''
if they have been modified. 
You can also run \fLyppull\fP\|(8) to kick
any slave servers which might not be immediately reachable.
.LP
If the old master does come back up and is still supposed to be a
\fLyp\fP server, the easiest way
to make it consistent with the new world is to run \fLypinit\fP\|(8)
with the \f(LB -s\fP, configuring the old master as a slave and drawing
the current database from the new master. 
This is a sort of blunt-instrument approach, but is easier 
than the other available alternatives.
.LP
\fBMaster Converts To Slave\fP \(em
It is easier to change from one master server to another when the old
master will continue to run. 
Make whatever changes are required to ``ypmaps'' 
and ``ypservers'' on the old master, and run
\fLyppush\fP\|(8) on the old master. 
This will let the old master know
about the new versions, but once it finds out about the change of its
own status from the new copy of ``ypmaps'', it will not contact its
peers to tell them to get new versions of the maps from it.
Then run \fLypinit\fP\|(8) with a \f(LB -s\fP switch at the new master
to get the latest version of everything from the old master
to the new master.
Also make sure the ``sources'' like \fL/etc/passwd\fP, are copied to the
new master. 
Kill and restart \fLypserv\fP at the new master. 
It will then know it is the master. 
You should then run \fLyppush\fP\|(8) at
the new master to get the rest of slaves to know about the new master.
.bp
.NH 2 
Debugging A Yellow Pages Client
.LP
We divide this debugging section into two parts \(em first those
problems seen on a \fLyp\fP client, and then those problems seen on
a \fLyp\fP server.
.LP
Before trying to debug a yellow pages client, read the earlier section
in this chapter on how the \fLyp\fP works.
.NH 3 
On Client: Commands Hang
.LP
The most common problem at a \fLyp\fP client node is for a command
to hang and generate console messages which say:
.LS 0
yp: server not responding for domain <wigwam>. Still trying
.LF
Sometimes many commands will begin to hang, even though the system as
a whole seems okay and you can run new commands.
.LP
The message above indicates that \fLypbind\fP on the local machine is
unable to communicate with \fLypserv\fP in the domain ``wigwam''. 
This often happens when machines which run \fLypserv\fP have crashed. 
It may also occur if the net or the \fLyp\fP server machine is so
overloaded that \fLypserv\fP can't get a response back to 
your \fLypbind\fP within the timeout period. 
Under these circumstances, all the other \fLyp\fP
client nodes on your net will show the same or similar problems.
The condition is temporary in most cases, and the messages will usually
go away when the \fLyp\fP server machine reboots and \fLypserv\fP gets
back in business; or when the load on the \fLyp\fP server nodes and/or
the Ethernet decreases.
.LP
However, in the circumstances described below, the situation will never
get better.
.IP \(bu
If the \fLyp\fP client has not set, or incorrectly set,
\fLdomainname\fP on the machine. 
Clients must use a domain name that the \fLyp\fP servers know. 
Use \fLdomainname\fP\|(1) to see the client domain name. 
Compare that with the domain name set on the \fLyp\fP servers.  
The domain name should be set in \fL/etc/rc.local\fP. 
When \fL/etc/rc.local\fP fails to set, or
incorrectly sets, \fLdomainname\fP you will have to reboot your system;
reboot it single-user (type \fLb -s\fP to the prom monitor).
Then edit \fL/etc/rc.local\fP to set the name correctly.
Type \fL<ctrl d>\fP to come up multi-user, and the problem should be
gone.
.IP \(bu
If your domain name is correct, make sure your local net has at
least one \fLyp\fP server machine. 
You can only bind to a \fLypserv\fP
process on your local net, not on another accessible net. 
There must
be at least one \fLyp\fP server for your machine's domain running on
your local net. 
Two or more \fLyp\fP servers will improve availability
and reponse characteristics for \fLyp\fP services.
.bp
.IP \(bu
If your local net has a \fLyp\fP server, make sure it is up and
running. 
Check other machines on your local net. 
If several
client machines have problems simultaneously, suspect a server problem.
Find a client machine behaving normally, and try the \fLypwhich\fP
command. 
If \fLypwhich\fP never returns an answer, kill it and go
to a terminal on the \fLyp\fP server machine. 
Type:
.LS
% \f(LBps ax | grep yp\fP
.LF
and look for \fLypserv\fP and \fLypbind\fP processes. 
If the server's
\fLypbind\fP daemon is not running, start it up by typing:
.LS
% \f(LB/etc/ypbind\fP
.LF
If there is a \fLypserv\fP process running, do a \fLypwhich\fP on the
\fLyp\fP server machine. 
If \fLypwhich\fP returns no answer,
\fLypserv\fP has probably hung and should be restarted.
Kill the existing \fLypserv\fP process (you must
be logged on as root), and start /etc/ypserv:
.LS
% \f(LBkill -9\fP [some pid # from ps]
% \f(LB/etc/ypserv\fP
.LF
If \fLps\fP shows no \fLypserv\fP process running, start one up.
.NH 3 
On Client: \fLyp\fP Service Unavailable
.LP
When other machines on the network appear to be okay, but
\fLyp\fP service becomes unavailable on your machine, many
different symptoms may show up. 
Among them: some commands appear to
operate correctly while others terminate printing an error message
about the unavailability of \fLyp\fP; some commands limp along in a
backup-strategy-mode particular to the program involved; and some
commands or daemons crash with obscure messages or no message at all.
For example, things like the following may show up:
.LS
my_machine% \f(LBypcat myfile\fP
ypcat: can't bind to \fLyp\fP server for domain <wigwam>.
	Reason: can't communicate with ypbind.
.LF
.LS
my_machine% \f(LB/etc/yp/yppoll myfile\fP
Sorry, I can't make use of the yellow pages. I give up.
.LF
.LP
When symptoms like those above occur, try
.LS
my_machine% \f(LBls -l\fP
.LF
on a directory containing files owned by many users, including users
not in the local machine's \fL/etc/passwd\fP file \(em for example
\fL/usr2\fP. 
If the \fLls -l\fP reports file owners not in the local
machine's \fL/etc/passwd\fP file as numbers, rather than names, it is
one more symptom that \fLyp\fP service is not working.
.LP
These symptoms usually indicate that your \fLypbind\fP process is not
running. 
You can do a \fLps ax\fP to check for one. 
If it you do not find it, type:
.LS
my_machine% \f(LB/etc/ypbind\fP
.LF
to start it. \fLyp\fP problems should disappear.
.bp
.NH 3 
On Client: \fLypbind\fP Crashes
.LP
If \fLypbind\fP crashes almost immediately each time it is started,
you should look for a problem in some other part of the system.
Check for the presence of the \fLportmap\fP daemon by typing:
.LS
my_machine%  \f(LBps ax | grep portmap\fP
.LF
If you don't find it running, reboot.
.LP
If \fLportmap\fP itself will
not stay up or behaves strangely, look for more fundamental problems.
Check the network software in the ways suggested in
the section on \fIEthernet Debugging\fP in the \fICommunications\fP
chapter of this manual.
.LP
You may be able to talk to the \fLportmap\fP on your machine from a
machine operating normally. 
From such a machine, type:
.LS
flipper% \f(LBrpcinfo -p\fP \fIyour_machine_name\fP
.LF
 If your \fLportmap\fP is okay, the output should look like:
.LS
[program, version, protocol, port]:

[100005, 1, 17, 1046]
[100001, 2, 17, 1055]
[100001, 1, 17, 1055]
[100002, 1, 17, 1052]
[100008, 1, 17, 1049]
[100007, 1, 17, 1027]
[100007, 1, 6, 1026]
.LF
On your machine the port numbers will be different.
The two entries that represent the \fLypbind\fP process are:
.LS
[100007, 1, 17, \fIport_#\fP]
[100007, 1, 6, \fIport_#\fP]
.LF
If they are not there, \fLypbind\fP has been unable to register its
services. 
Reboot the machine. 
If they are there and they change each
time you try to restart \fL/etc/ypbind\fP, reboot the system, even if
the \fLportmap\fP is up.
If the situation persists after reboot, call for help.
.\".H 4 "On Client: Software Development Errors"
.\".LP
.\"When writing programs that query the \fLyp\fP database, certain
.\"problems arise.
.\"Typically, the program won't find the data you want where
.\"you expect it. Possibly you have used an invalid map name, or have
.\"called the wrong map, or have specified a key value not in the map.
.\".LP
.\"First make sure the map name is one \fLyp\fP knows about.
.\"attempt to figure out the problem should be to use ypcat to see if the
.\"mapname is valid; that is, it is one which the \fLyp\fP knows about.  Pipe ypcat's
.\"output to grep, and search for the key you're looking for.  (Remember that
.\"ypcat's default is to not print out the map keys.  Depending on the
.\"structure of the map, it may be appropriate to use the "-k" flag to ypcat
.\"when searching for a key.) For example, let's assume that mapname "foo" is
.\"invalid:
.\".LS
.\"my_machine% ypcat -k foo | grep bar
.\"ypcat: can't get first record from yp. Reason: no such map in server's domain.
.\".LE
.\"If mapname foo were valid, ypcat would not print out the error message, but
.\"would rather print the contents of the map to its standard output.  grep may
.\"or may not find the entry you are looking for.  If the key you're looking
.\"for is not there, check that you are using the correct map.  For instance,
.\"if you are searching for a user's name, you must specify map
.\""passwd.byname", and a request to "passwd.byuid" won't find the key you
.\"supply.  If the map is one which is not supplied by Sun, make sure the data
.\"base generation process is correct; that, for instance, the procedure for
.\"creating an input file for makedbm is going to result in an entry with the
.\"key-value pair you are expecting to find later.  You can check the structure
.\"of the final map by using makedbm with the "-u" flag on the master yp
.\"server.  For instance:
.\".LS
.\"ypmaster%  cd /etc/yp
.\"ypmaster%  makedbm -u <my_domain>/<my_map> 
.\".LE
.\"will dump out the contents of map my_map in domain my_domain.  Voluminous
.\"output can, of course, be piped to grep.  If you can't find the entry you
.\"are looking for by this method, neither will the \fLyp\fP be able to.  If you are
.\"using one of the standard data bases, like the hosts data base (which was
.\"implemented by the file /etc/hosts), the standard library functions
.\"(gethostbyname, getgrgid(is this a typo for getgrpid), getpw, ...) know about the correct mapname for
.\"each desired translation.  You should use these standard library functions
.\"if at all possible.  It goes without saying (therefore, I'll say it) that
.\"you shouldn't be doing file I/O on the ASCII files within /etc; the data
.\"isn't there any more - it's in the yp.
.\".LP
.\"If you can get data back from the yp, but the data is inconsistent from one
.\"run of your program to another, or the program generates different results
.\"depending on where it's run, the problem is probably one of a data base
.\"version skew among the several \fLyp\fP servers.  Please look at the section below
.\"which discusses data base propagation and skew.
.NH 3 
On Client: \fLypwhich\fP Inconsistent
.LP
When you use \fLypwhich\fP several times at the same client node, the
answer you get back varies \(em the \fLyp\fP server changes.
This is normal. 
The binding of \fLyp\fP client to \fLyp\fP server
will change over time on a busy net, and when the \fLyp\fP servers are
busy. 
Whenever possible, the system stabilizes at a point where all
clients get acceptable response time from the \fLyp\fP servers.
As long as your client machine gets \fLyp\fP service, it doesn't
matter where the service comes from. 
Often a \fLyp\fP server
machine gets its own \fLyp\fP services from another
\fLyp\fP server on the net.
.bp
.NH 2 
Debugging A Yellow Pages Server
.LP
Before trying to debug a yellow pages server, read the earlier section
in this chapter on how the \fLyp\fP works.
.NH 3 
On Server: Different Versions Of A \fLyp\fP Map
.LP
Since \fLyp\fP works by propagating maps between servers, you will
commonly find different versions of a map at different servers on the
network. 
This version skew is normal, if transient, and
abnormal otherwise. 
How do we define transient? 
Let's look for a
minute at the \fLyp\fP update-propagation algorithms.
.LP
Each map must be updated at its master \fLyp\fP server. 
Typically, all maps have the same master \fLyp\fP 
server \(em \fLypinit\fP\|(8) assumes this standard.
.LP
\fLyppush\fP\|(8) should always be run on the master server after you
update a database. 
When you use the Makefile in \fL/etc/yp\fP,
\fLyppush\fP runs automatically. \fLyppush\fP tells the \fLypserv\fP
process at the master about the new copy of the map. \fLypserv\fP then
reinitializes its information about
the map and contacts its non-master peer \fLypserv\fP processes (in
other words, the \fLyp\fP slave servers) with news of the update.
After this contact, the new map is transferred from master to slave.
.LP
During the process there will, of course, be a temporary version skew.
Sometimes this skew will drag out for a long time. 
For example, a
heavily loaded slave server, or a slave on a busy net, may not get the
update message; or, the \fLypserv\fP process may be swapped out with
many previous requests in its queue; or, the slave server's
\fLypserv\fP process may be down, or the slave's machine may be down.
In these cases, the message sent from the master to the slave may time
out or get dumped by the system. 
The master doesn't keep track of who
got the message and who didn't. 
So if a slave misses the first try, it
misses it altogether.
.LP
How does the slave get the updated map in the circumstances
described above?  
Each slave server continuously scans its list of
known maps, and tries to find some other \fLyp\fP server with a more
recent version. 
If it finds one, it gets that more recent version from
its peer. 
It always first attempts to communicate with the map's
master, but if the master is unavailable it chooses another peer at
random. 
The combination of the master server ``pushing'' and the slave
servers ``scanning for'' updated files will eventually bring map
versions into sync. 
A \fLyp\fP server traverses its lists of all known
maps in somewhat less than half-an-hour. 
In addition, a user may run
\fLyppull\fP\|(8) to tell a slave to seek a new version of a map. 
It is similar to \fLyppush\fP, since it hurries the system up, but it does
not guarantee success - it's a ``hint'' to the system.
.LP
A master server scans its list of known maps in a similar manner.
But instead of communicating with its peers to find a more recent
version, it rechecks the disk files which implement the maps, and
tries to find version changes by itself. 
It also detects when new
maps come into existence. 
In both cases, when it finds an updated or new map, it contacts 
its slaves exactly as if \fLyppush\fP had been run.
.LP
Therefore, if you detect a map version skew between two \fLyp\fP
servers (using \fLyppoll\fP\|(8), for example) run \fLyppull\fP\|(8),
and check back on in about ten minutes. 
Run it a second time if you don't see results. 
If you try a few times and nothing happens you
should suspect something is broken.
.bp
.LP
Propagation failures can be caused by errors in the databases \fLyp\fP
uses to run itself. 
Make sure that all \fLyp\fP servers in
question are mentioned in the map ``ypservers'', both within the default
domain and the domain ``yp_private''. 
Also make sure there are entries
in the map ``hosts.byname'' within both domains.
.LP
If none of these solves the problem, you can work around it by using
\fLrcp\fP or \fLtftp\fP to copy those \fLdbm\fP files that implement
the map, from the master to the out-of-sync slave. 
Make sure that the
\fLdbm\fP files end up with ``root'' as the owner.
.NH 3 
On Server: \fLypserv\fP Crashes
.LP
When the \fLypserv\fP process crashes almost immediately, and won't
stay up even with repeated activations, the debug process is virtually
identical to that described above in the section \fIOn Client: ypbind
Crashes\fP. 
Check for the \fLportmap\fP daemon:
.LS
ypserver% \f(LBps ax | grep portmap\fP
.LF
Reboot the server if you do not find it. 
If it is there, type:
.LS
ypserver% \f(LB/usr/etc/rpcinfo -p\fP \fIypserver_name\fP
.LF
and look for output to the screen like:
.LS
[program, version, protocol, port]:

[100001, 2, 17, 1062]
[100001, 1, 17, 1062]
[100002, 1, 17, 1060]
[100008, 1, 17, 1058]
[100005, 1, 17, 1056]
[100007, 1, 17, 1032]
[100007, 1, 6, 1027]
[100004, 1, 6, 1026]
[100004, 1, 17, 1024]
.LF
On your machine, the port numbers will be different. 
The two entries
that represent the \fLypserv\fP process are:
.LS
[100004, 1, 6, \fIport_#\fP]
[100004, 1, 17, \fIport_#\fP]
.LF
If they are not there, \fLypserv\fP has been unable to register its
services. 
Reboot the machine. 
If they are there, and they change each
time you try to restart \fL/etc/ypserv\fP, reboot the machine.
If the situation persists after reboot, call for help.
.bp
.NH 2 
Yellow Pages Policies
.LP
Here are the policies set by the C-library routines when they access
the following files on a system running the yellow pages.
.IP "\fL/etc/passwd\fP"
Always consulted. 
If there are \fL+\fP or \fL-\fP entries,
the \fLyp\fP password map is consulted, otherwise \fLyp\fP is unused.
.IP "\fL/etc/group\fP"
Always consulted. 
If there are \fL+\fP or \fL-\fP entries,
the \fLyp\fP group map is consulted, otherwise \fLyp\fP is unused.
.IP "\fL/etc/hosts.equiv\fP"
(And similarly for \fL.rhosts\fP) Always consulted, though neither
of these files is in the yellow pages database. 
(See the section
below \fIHow Security Is Changed With The Yellow Pages\fP, for
a fuller explanation of these two files.) 
If there are
\fL+\fP or \fL-\fP entries, whose arguments are netgroups, the \fLyp\fP
netgroup map is consulted, otherwise \fLyp\fP is unused.
.IP "\fL/etc/services\fP"
Never consulted. 
The data that was formerly read from
this file now comes from the \fLyp\fP services database.
.IP "\fL/etc/protocols\fP"
Never consulted. 
The data that was formerly read from
this file now comes from the \fLyp\fP protocols database.
.IP "\fL/etc/networks\fP"
Never consulted. 
The data that was formerly read from
this file now comes from the \fLyp\fP networks database.
.IP "\fL/etc/netgroup\fP"
Never consulted. 
The data that was formerly read from
this file now comes from the \fLyp\fP netgroup database.
.IP "\fL/etc/hosts\fP"
Consulted only when booting (by the \fLifconfig\fP command in the
\fL/etc/rc.local\fP file). 
After that the \fLyp\fP is used instead.
.NH 2 
How Security Is Changed With The Yellow Pages
.LP
Read the section above on \fLyp\fP accessing policies to better
understand \fLyp\fP security issues.
.bp
.NH 3 
Global And Local \fLyp\fP Database Files
.LP
There are seven files now in the yellow pages database. 
Six were formerly in \fL/etc\fP\|: \fL/etc/passwd\fP,
\fL/etc/group\fP, \fL/etc/hosts\fP, \fL/etc/networks\fP,
\fL/etc/services\fP, and \fL/etc/protocols\fP.
In addition, there is a new file \fL/etc/netgroup\fP.
(Note that a site may add database files of its own.)
We divide the yellow pages into local and global file types.
A local file is first checked for on your own machine, then in the
yellow pages. 
A global file is only checked for in the yellow pages.
\fL/etc/passwd\fP and \fL/etc/group\fP are the local files in the
yellow pages database. 
The other five yellow pages files are global.
.LP
For example, a program that calls \fL/etc/passwd\fP (a local file)
will first look in the password file on your machine;
the yellow pages password file will only be consulted
if your machine's password file contains ``\fL+\fP'' (plus sign)
entries. 
The \fL/etc/passwd\fP file is local so that you can control
the entries for your own machine. 
The only other local file is \fL/etc/group\fP. 
To repeat, local files are consulted first on your
own machine, before looking in the yellow pages.
.LP
The remaining yellow pages files (\fLhosts\fP, \fLnetworks\fP,
\fLservices\fP, \fLprotocols\fP,
and \fLnetgroup\fP\|) are global files. 
The information in these files
is network wide data, and in a perfect world
would only be accessed from the yellow pages.  
However, there are two situations when it is necessary
to consult a copy of these files on your own machine.
The first assists in the changeover to the yellow pages. 
If someone
installs 2.0 software, but a yellow pages server has not yet been
started up, then no network data will be available.
Second, when booting, each machine needs an entry
in \fL/etc/hosts\fP for itself.
In summary, if yellow pages is running, global files are only checked
in the yellow pages; a file on your local machine is not consulted.
.NH 3 
Two Other Files \fLyp\fP Consults
.LP
The files \fL/etc/hosts.equiv\fP and \fL/.rhosts\fP are not in the
yellow pages database. 
Each machine has its own unique copy. 
However it is possible to put entries in your \fL/etc/hosts.equiv\fP
file that refer to the yellow pages. 
For example a line consisting of
.LS
+@engineering
.LF
will include all members of \fLengineering\fP as it is defined in the
local file \fL/etc/netgroup\fP or in the \fLyp\fP database.
A line consisting only of ``\fL+\fP'' (a plus sign) will include
everyone in your \fL/etc/hosts.equiv\fP file.
.NH 3 
Security Implications
.LP
Recall that to be able to log into a machine without having a password,
you need to be in both the \fL/etc/hosts.equiv\fP file and
the \fL/etc/passwd\fP file. 
By having a ``\fL+\fP'' entry
in \fL/etc/hosts.equiv\fP, you effectively bypass this check, and
anyone in your \fL/etc/passwd\fP file will be allowed to rlogin to
your machine without restriction.
.bp
.LP
An \fL/etc/passwd\fP file and \fL/etc/group\fP file may also
have ``\fL+\fP'' entries.  
A line in an \fL/etc/passwd\fP file such as
.LS
+nb::::Napoleon Bonaparte:/usr2/nb:/bin/csh
.LF
pulls in an entry for \fLnb\fP from the yellow pages. 
It gets
the \fLuid\fP, \fLgid\fP and \fLpassword\fP from the yellow pages, and
gets the gecos, home directory and default shell from the ``\fL+\fP''
entry itself. 
On the other hand, an \fL/etc/passwd\fP entry such as
.LS
+nb:
.LF
gets all information from the yellow pages.
.LP
Finally, notice that
.LS
+nb::1189:10:Napoleon Bonaparte:/usr2/nb:/bin/csh
.LF
is quite different from
.LS
nb::1189:10:Napoleon Bonaparte:/usr2/nb:/bin/csh
.LF
In the first of the two examples the password field is obtained from
the yellow pages, in the second user \fLnb\fP has no password.
Also, if there is no entry for \fLnb\fP in the yellow pages, then
the effect of the first example is as if no entry for \fLnb\fP
was present at all. 
That is quite different from the second example.
.NH 3 
Special \fLyp\fP Password Change
.LP
When you change your password with the \fLpasswd\fP\|(1) command, you
will change the entry explicitly given in your own \fL/etc/passwd\fP
file.  
If your password is not given explicitly, but rather is pulled
in from the yellow pages with a ``\fL+\fP'' entry, then the
\fLpasswd\fP command will print the error message \fLNot in passwd
file\fP. 
To change your passwd in the yellow pages, you must use the
new \fLyppasswd\fP\|(1) command. 
In order to enable this service,
the system administrator must start up the daemon \fLyppasswdd\fP\|(8C)
server on the machine serving as the master for the yellow pages
password file.
.NH 3 
Manual Pages Covering Security Issues
.LP
For more details, see the following man pages: \fLyppasswd\fP\|(1),
\fLhosts.equiv\fP\|(5),
\fLpasswd\fP\|(5), \fLgroup\fP\|(5), \fLnetgroup\fP\|(5),
\fLyppasswdd\fP\|(8C).
.NH 2 
What If You Do Not Use The Yellow Pages?
.LP
If you choose not to use the yellow pages, the procedure for bypassing
the software implementation is quite simple. 
In the file \fL/etc/rc.local\fP find the lines that look like:
.LS
if [ -f /etc/ypbind ]; then
	/etc/ypbind; echo -n ' ypbind'	>/dev/console
fi
.LF
And comment them out, like:
.LS
# if [ -f /etc/ypbind ]; then
#	  /etc/ypbind; echo -n ' ypbind'	>/dev/console
# fi
.LF
.bp
.NH
Adding A New User To A Machine
.LP
To add a new user, add an entry to the password file and create a home
directory on the new user's machine as described in the steps below.
To add a new client machine, see \fIAdding A Client Machine To An
\fLnd\fP \fIServer\fP, below.
.NH 2 
Edit The Master \fLyp\fP Server's \fL/etc/passwd\fP File
.LP
Typically, for a new user, add a password file entry to every
machine on the local network. 
You must be superuser to do it, and you
should begin on the master \fLyp\fP server machine.
First edit the master \fLyp\fP server's \fL/etc/passwd\fP file. 
Later the password file entry for the user will be copied to the
\fL/etc/passwd\fP file on the new client's partition; without
an entry in it, the person administering the new client machine
would not be able to login should the yellow pages fail.
.LP
On the master \fLyp\fP server add a new line to the password file
with the \fLvipw\fP command; \fLvipw\fP brings the password file into
the \fLvi\fP editor, and prevents anyone else from editing it
until you are done:
.LS
# \f(LB/etc/vipw\fP
.LF
\fL/etc/passwd\fP is a readable ASCII file with a one line entry for
each valid user on the system. 
Each entry is separated into fields by
colons (:)\^; there are seven fields on each line and some fields may
be left blank by placing two colons
back to back. 
Avoid using the characters for single and double
quotes (` ' "), and backslashes (\e) in the password file.
See \fLpasswd\fP\|(5) for more about the file format.
.LP
Let us suppose that your new user's name is Mr. Chimp
and his account is going to be ``bonzo'', you would add a line similar
to the one shown below.
.LS
bonzo::1947:10:Mr. Chimp:/usr/bonzo:/bin/csh
.LF
.LP
Notice that the second field is blank in the example.
This field, when filled, contains an encrypted version of the user's
password. 
However, when the field is blank, anyone can login
simply by typing the user name \(em no password is required. 
You cannot
create a password by making an entry in the \fL/etc/passwd\fP
file. 
You must use \fLpasswd\fP\|(1) while logged in as the user, or
as superuser. 
Since anyone can login when a user has no password,
you may provide a password for the new user and let him know it so he
can log in and change it to whatever he prefers. 
When Mr. Chimp logs in
for the first time, he can use the \fLpasswd\fP utility to change his
password, or \fLyppasswd\fP\|(1) to change it in the \fLyp\fP
database.
.LP
After Mr. Chimp has a password, the entry for ``bonzo'' in the
password file will look something like:
.LS
bonzo:Sv9wEuIq:1947:10:Mr. Chimp:/usr/bonzo:/bin/csh
.LF
Fields in the password file have the following meanings:
.RS
.IP 1)
Login name \(em synonymous with user name.
.bp
.IP 2)
Encrypted password. 
Tell all new users how to add, or change, their
password with the \fLpasswd\fP command and the \fLyppasswd\fP command.
Remember, the system administrator can make this field empty
when a user has forgotten his or her password, thereby enabling
login without a password until such time as a new one is given.
.IP 3)
User ID. 
A number unique to this user. 
A system knows the user by ID
number associated with login name, therefore a login name must have the
same user ID number on all password files of machines which are
networked in a local domain. 
Failure to keep ID's unique will prevent
moving files between directories on different machines because the
system will respond as if the directories are owned by two different
users. 
In addition, file ownership may become confused when an \s-2NFS\s0
server exports a directory to an \s-2NFS\s0
client whose password file contains users with uid's that match those
of different users on the \s-2NFS\s0 server.
.IP 4)
Group ID. 
Can be used to group users together who are working
on similar projects. 
All system staff are in
group ``10'' for historical reasons. 
In this example Mr. Chimp is in
the system staff group. 
Do not put normal users in this group. 
If you
are not sure what group to put a new client in, see \fLgroup\fP\|(5)
and look in the file \fL/etc/group\fP.
.IP 5)
Information about user \(em usually real name, phone number, etc. 
An ampersand (&) here is shorthand for the user's login name.
.IP 6)
The user's home directory \(em the directory the user logs in to.
.IP 7)
Initial shell to use on login. 
If this field is blank the default \fL/bin/sh\fP is used. 
We recommend placing \fL/bin/csh\fP here, as in the example above, 
it gives a Berkeley 4.2 C-Shell as the user's initial shell.
.RE
.LP
After you have updated the password file and created a password for
the new user, be sure to update the yellow database by 
running \fL/etc/yp/make\fP for \fL/etc/passwd\fP:
.LS
# \f(LBcd /etc/yp\fP
# \f(LBmake /etc/passwd\fP
.LF
.NH 2 
Make A Home Directory
.LP
After adding a new entry to the password file, you should create a
home directory for the new user to login to. 
This will be the same as
the directory given in the sixth field of the password file entry.
In the \fL/usr2\fP directory make
a directory for the new user and change ownership to the user's
login name and change group to the user's group. 
For example:
.LS
# \f(LBcd /usr2\fP
# \f(LBmkdir bonzo\fP
# \f(LBchown bonzo bonzo\fP
# \f(LBchgrp 10 bonzo\fP
.LF
.LP
Note that if the yellow pages databases for the password file have not
yet been updated on the machine's yellow pages server, you will get the
following error message when you attempt to do the \fLchown\fP:
.LS
unknown user id:  username
.LF
.bp
.LP
In that case, you can use the following set of commands:
.LS
# \f(LBcd /usr2\fP
# \f(LBmkdir bonzo\fP
# \f(LBchown\fP \fIuserid#\fP \f(LBbonzo\fP
# \f(LBchgrp 10 bonzo\fP
.LF
You use Mr. Chimp's user id number (from the password file entry)
instead of login name to change the ownership of his home directory.
.NH 2 
The New User's Environment
.LP
Finally, you may define the new user's environment on login in several
ways. 
For example you may give her a copy of such files as \fL.login\fP
and \fL.cshrc\fP if she uses ``/bin/csh'', or \fL.profile\fP
if she uses ``/bin/sh''. 
See the \fLcsh\|\fP(1) and \fLsh\fP\|(1) pages
in the \fICommands Reference Manual\fP for discussion of these files,
they can automatically set up the terminal and shell environment at
each login. 
You may also want to give a user \fL.suntools\fP and \fL.mailrc\fP files.
.LP
If the new user is a member of any groups at your site, add her to
\fL/etc/group\fP as necessary \(em see \fLgroup\fP\|(5) and
\fLgroups\fP\|(1).  
Be sure to make the changes to the \fL/etc/group\fP
file on the master \fLyp\fP server if you run the yellow pages.
